<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reichenau Island</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Reichenau is an island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>, in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, joined to the mainland by a causeway. The Benedictine abbey founded there by the wandering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> Pirmin in 724 became a major centre of learning, book painting and church building under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a> and Ottonians. Its three Romanesque churches have been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> since 2000.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reichenau-island.jpg" alt="Reichenau Island" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Steiger · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:InselReichenau_20090709_MartinSteiger_CCBYSA_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Reichenau lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a> almost due west of the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanz">Konstanz</a>, between the Gnadensee and the Untersee, the two parts of the lake. The island has a land area of 4.3 square kilometres and a circumference of 11 kilometres. It is 4.5 kilometres long and 1.5 kilometres wide at its widest, and its highest point, the Hochwart, stands about 43 metres above the lake. A causeway completed in 1838 connects it to the mainland, crossed by a waterway, the Bruckgraben, which a low road bridge spans.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>People lived on Reichenau in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, but archaeological evidence suggests that it was abandoned in the Roman era and stayed empty until 724. In that year the monk Pirmin received the support of the Carolingian ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-martel">Charles Martel</a> to build a monastery. The first monastery, at Mittelzel, was of wood and had been replaced in stone by 746.</p><p>In the early 9th century the community flourished under the patronage of the Carolingian and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottonian-dynasty">Ottonian dynasty</a>. In 816 the monastery church was rebuilt as a cruciform basilica. Two further churches were built, for Saints Peter and Paul, consecrated in 799, and for Saint George, in 896. Relics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-the-evangelist">Saint Mark</a> arrived at the abbey in the middle of the 9th century.</p><p>The abbey produced poets and authors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walafrid-strabo">Walafrid Strabo</a>, who served as abbot, and scholars such as Hermann of Reichenau and Abbot Reginbert, as well as artists. The Ottonian murals of the miracles of Christ in the church of Saint George are unique survivals from the 10th century, and the Plan of St. Gall, the only surviving architectural drawing from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, may also have been made on the island.</p><p>In the 16th century the territory of the Prince-Bishopric of Constance was extended to include Reichenau, and the abbey's influence waned. When the Prince-Bishopric was secularised in 1803, many smaller chapels were demolished, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and archives went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-university">University of Heidelberg</a> library, and the surrounding farms were divided up and sold.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The three churches and the abbey were declared a World Heritage Site in 2000. The abbey's bailiff lived in a two-storey stone house to which two more storeys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> framing were added in the 14th century, one of the oldest timber-frame buildings in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, now a museum of local history. The island is also known for its vegetable farms, and the Wollmatinger Ried beside it is a large reed wetland and nature reserve where many birds stop on their annual migration.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Reichenau Island” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Christianity spread through bishoprics at Constance and Basel and monasteries such as St Gall, founded on the hermitage of Gallus, and <b>Reichenau</b>, founded in 724.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-alamannia">Duchy of Alamannia</a></li><li><blockquote>In 724 the wandering bishop Pirmin founded a Benedictine monastery on the island of <b>Reichenau</b> in Lake Constance, with the support of Charles Martel.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-reichenau">Imperial Abbey of Reichenau</a></li><li><blockquote>The bishops acquired the <b>Reichenau</b> abbey in 1540.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-constance">Prince-Bishopric of Constance</a></li></ul></section>
